<file path="findings.md">
# Portal performance findings — 12 October 2026

What this repo does: The portal manages student accounts, project teams, supervisor capacity, document review, fines, messages, and Viva scheduling and grades. Next.js API routes read and write MongoDB through Mongoose; browsers transfer PDFs and audio directly to S3-compatible storage.

The assumed workload is the supplied baseline: 1,000 students, 500 supervisors, 500 initial projects, 100 distinct Viva sessions, and concurrent account/project operations. The next verification should include 50% headroom: 75 concurrent requests per 50-request operation and 150 distinct sessions. These are verification targets, not a claim that production supports those numbers.

This is a report-only audit. Application code, database records, indexes, buckets, and deployment settings were not changed. This lowercase file is a new audit of current source; the older [Findings.md](Findings.md) remains separate.

## What the evidence establishes

The portal completed the baseline correctly, but several operations took seconds. Passing means the expected requests and data checks succeeded; it does not mean response times were acceptable.

| Operation | Baseline p95 | What the current source makes it do |
| --- | ---: | --- |
| PDF reservation | 11,033 ms | Authentication, policy and fine checks, rate-limit writes, a quota transaction, URL signing |
| PDF submission | 6,918 ms | Repeat checks, storage verification and copying, project/policy/storage/outbox transaction, activity write |
| Project reset | 5,485 ms | Project read, shared-file checks, deletion enqueue, project update, activity write |
| Team change | 5,276 ms | Invite/team validation, old-project cleanup, capacity release, membership writes, activity write |
| Supervisor change | 4,862 ms | Capacity reservation, old-project cleanup, replacement project, activity write |
| Second Viva start wave | 4,584 ms | Current participant validation, participant locks, snapshot/session writes, audit event |
| Grade save | 3,366 ms | Authentication, guarded session update, audit event and commit |
| Student dashboard | 3,092 ms | Account/access checks, project, policy, results, schedules, supervisor and team |
| Viva dashboard | 2,695 ms | Account/access checks, panel/session reads; live context for scheduled sessions |
| Login, CSRF + callback | 2,327 ms | Password verification, account/access reads and successful-login writes |
| Local PDF transfer | 7 ms | A direct PUT of a **69-byte** synthetic file |

Source: [testing.md](testing.md) and the local `.local-bench/portal-load-2026-10-12/report.json`. I recalculated the HTTP count: 900, excluding the combined CSRF/callback metric to avoid counting the same requests twice. The report records no dropped requests or unexpected responses.

Three evidence levels are used below:

- **Current-source confirmed:** the unnecessary work, contention point, or reliability defect is visible in the checked-out code. This does not establish its exact share of elapsed time.
- **Previously measured:** the 6 October diagnostic artifacts measured a mechanism that still exists. That was a different workload and build, so its timings are supporting evidence, not measurements of 12 October.
- **Needs measurement:** a source-backed candidate whose importance requires a query plan or isolated comparison.

The 12 October report has `phases: []`. It contains endpoint timings, not per-query durations, connection waiting, transaction retry counts, CPU stacks, or object-storage call timings. Therefore, it cannot prove that one particular database query caused all 11 seconds of reservation latency.

### Two corrections to the baseline interpretation

1. In `tests/support/portal-load-baseline-runner.mjs:201`, the runner **awaits the entire login wave** before creating the other actions at lines 244–267. The login wave carries the `mixed-load` label, but it does not overlap those later actions. Password hashing during password-reset completion does overlap later mixed work.
2. The upload fixture at line 231 is only **69 bytes**. The 7 ms transfer measures loopback overhead for a tiny object, not a real proposal or thesis. Reservation and submission are demonstrably slow in that test; production PDF transfer speed remains unmeasured.

The later project/account actions, dashboard reads, grade saves, and second start wave are launched together. Initial starts had p95 589 ms; second starts had p95 4,584 ms. This is evidence of load sensitivity, but the two waves are not an isolated experiment. Starts do **not** increment a shared round counter in the current implementation.

## How requests and data move through the portal

### Page generation and browser calls

`app/page.tsx` is a client component. It waits for the NextAuth session, dynamically loads the role dashboard, and that dashboard fetches its data. The main dashboard content is not generated with database data on the server before the browser receives the page.

| Screen or event | Browser calls | Data/work returned |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Root page and browser focus | `/api/auth/session` through SessionProvider; `/api/portal-status`; `/api/registration-policy` | Session identity, maintenance status, public registration settings |
| Student Home | `/api/dashboard/student`; `/api/headline`; `/api/dashboard/viva-access` | Student, full current project, members, supervisor/broadcast, fines/policy, Viva results and schedules, headline, access status |
| Student supervisor selection | `/api/supervisors`, loaded when needed | Supervisor identity and capacity catalogue |
| Student resources | `/api/templates?stage=…`, loaded when requested | Allowed stage's HTML Word-template bundle |
| Supervisor Home | `/api/dashboard/supervisor`; `/api/dashboard/viva-access`; then `/api/dashboard/supervisor/viva?preview=1` after Home mounts | All assigned projects and members, migration code, access status, three-item Viva response |
| Supervisor Viva workspace | `/api/dashboard/supervisor/viva` | Full available session workspaces; mutation responses already return the changed workspace |
| Admin Home | `/api/admin/summary`; `/api/headline` | Counts and review-queue total; management lists are deferred |
| Admin Students / Supervisors | `/api/admin/students`; `/api/admin/supervisors` when first opened | Cursor-paged students and metadata; full supervisor list |
| Admin reports / reviews / fines / logs | Their corresponding `/api/admin/…` endpoints when opened | Selected report, bounded review/fine lists, recent activity |
| Admin Viva | `/api/admin/viva?catalogue=1` initially; operational reloads use `catalogue=0` | Setup catalogue plus rounds, panels, schedules; later reads retain the setup catalogue in the browser |
| Communication | `/api/dashboard/student/message`, `/api/dashboard/messages`, supervisor broadcast endpoint; `/api/voice/upload` for audio | Current messages or routed staff inbox; audio follows the reservation/PUT/finalization protocol |
| View PDF/audio | `/api/read-pdf?url=…`, followed by redirect to storage | Permission check and signed GET URL; object bytes stream directly from storage |

Source: `app/page.tsx`, `app/Providers.tsx`, the three `components/dashboards/` components, and their hooks/API helpers. There is no continuous five-second dashboard polling in the current UI. Focus, visibility changes, section remounts, explicit refresh, and post-action refreshes can still issue reads.

### Shared authorization work

`lib/security/auth.ts:35` decodes the token and reads the active User, including `sessionVersion`. `requireCurrentUser` then checks the Viva participant lock and, for non-admins, maintenance status. The maintenance read is coalesced and cached for five seconds in each process; active-account and participant-lock checks remain database reads.

The proxy checks token/role and adds response security headers. It does not perform these database reads. Rechecking current account state in the API is necessary for revocation; this should not be replaced with trusting an old JWT alone.

### Storage and modification ownership

| Data | Canonical storage and modifications |
| --- | --- |
| Account identity, credentials, fines | `User`; passwords use asynchronous scrypt. Password changes increment `sessionVersion`. Registration snapshots the fine policy. Email changes use password/OTP checks. |
| Team membership, assigned supervisor, project details/status/stage/PDF/ratings | `Project`; membership is an array. Mutations use project version checks where implemented; supervisor capacity is maintained on User as `occupiedSlots`. |
| Registration and submission switches | A single `RegistrationPolicy` document. Registration/submission transactions also increment acceptance counters on this document. |
| Student registration | Claim normalized roll number, create User and Project, reserve supervisor capacity if selected, commit, append activity. |
| Supervisor change or academic reset | Detach from the old team or remove a sole-member project, queue unused files, release/reserve capacity, create a new project, save account changes as needed. |
| Join/leave team | Guard team capacity/membership, update old and target projects, remove an obsolete sole-member project where needed; leaving creates a new unassigned project. |
| Document review / stage progression | Guard stage/version/status, save project update/ratings, queue per-recipient notification emails in the transaction; no inline SMTP for these notifications. |
| Upload capacity | One `SystemConfig` record with `configKey: 'storage'`, `usedBytes`, and `reservedBytes`; `UploadReservation` holds owner, kind, size, state, expiry, idempotency key and final object key. |
| File deletion | `StorageDeletionOutbox`; queued objects continue to consume quota until the worker deletes them and adjusts the ledger. |
| Messages | Current student request/reply fields and supervisor broadcast fields live on User; VoiceNote remains involved in older project cleanup. Audio objects use the upload protocol. |
| Viva setup and schedules | `VivaRound`, `VivaPanel`, and `VivaSession`; panel/schedule revisions guard setup changes. Automatic scheduling reads overlapping reservations and revalidates on save. |
| Viva execution/results | Confirm/start snapshots round, project and people into VivaSession. Start creates unique `VivaParticipantLock` records; grade updates the session and writes an audit event; complete/cancel removes locks. |
| Activity and notifications | A single bounded recent-activity document; individual durable Viva audit records and EmailOutbox records. Daily cron runs cleanup and notification delivery. |

### Database work per request

These are source-derived logical operations on normal successful paths, not captured command counts. They exclude transaction commit/abort, cursor continuation, retries, cold connection/model setup, and an occasional maintenance-cache miss.

| Request | Approximate normal database work |
| --- | --- |
| Student dashboard | **8 reads** if schedule snapshots satisfy the request; **9** when fallback checks a project with no schedules; up to **12** with live schedule context |
| Running Viva dashboard | **4 reads** including authorization: account, lock, panels, sessions; scheduled context can add rounds, projects and people |
| Viva start | **10 operations** including authorization, four current-context reads, locks, session update and audit insert |
| Grade save | **4 operations** including authorization, guarded update and audit insert |
| Session cancel | **6 operations** including authorization, session read/update, lock removal and audit insert |
| PDF reservation, student with project, no outstanding fine | **13 operations** with account and IP rate-limit dimensions: auth 2, policy 1, limiter 2, student/project 2, team-fine project/member reads 2, reservation transaction 4 |

The reservation transaction has four operations: ledger readiness read, existing-reservation read, quota update, reservation insert. An outstanding fine can add another policy read; retries repeat transactional work. A new submission adds more checks and writes, detailed below.

The purpose of counting is to expose amplification: 50 browser actions can produce hundreds of database operations. One HTTP request does not equal one database call.

## Must fix

### 1. Some capacity and registration transactions fail on ordinary write conflicts

- **What this is:** `app/api/dashboard/student/route.ts:271,405`, `app/api/register/route.ts:151`, `app/api/dashboard/supervisor/route.ts:192,307`, and `app/api/delete-supervisor/route.ts:35` manually start and commit transactions.
- **Problem:** There is no transaction/commit retry handling on these paths. Two students selecting the same supervisor, or registrations sharing the policy document, can conflict; the routes return generic errors instead of rereading capacity and completing or returning the correct full-capacity response. This is current-source confirmed; the baseline used different target supervisors and did not test that collision.
- **Fix:** Reuse the existing `withStorageTransaction`/native `withTransaction` pattern with session cleanup in `finally`; keep external effects and activity writes after a successful commit. Preserve business failures, and handle invite-code collisions by restarting a transaction rather than continuing an already-aborted transaction in `lib/projectCreation.ts:24`.
- **If we skip it:** Concentrated demand produces avoidable failures and user retries, which add more load.

The convenient API includes retry handling; manual transactions require it explicitly. [MongoDB transaction documentation](https://www.mongodb.com/docs/drivers/node/current/crud/transactions/).

### 2. Some Viva paths run parallel queries in one transaction

- **What this is:** `lib/vivaSessionDashboard.ts:521–530` runs round, panel and project queries using the same transaction session inside `Promise.all`. `lib/vivaRoundAdmin.ts:204–213` does the same during confirmation snapshots.
- **Problem:** Parallel operations within one transaction are unsupported. A passing fixture does not make that execution pattern reliable under load; errors/retries can also waste work. This is a confirmed implementation defect, not proof that it caused the baseline timings.
- **Fix:** Await those queries sequentially when a transaction session is present. Keep independent non-transactional dashboard reads parallel; no replacement library or abstraction is required.
- **If we skip it:** Start/confirmation behavior depends on unsupported driver usage during the most sensitive assessment actions.

See [Mongoose's transaction parallelism rule](https://mongoosejs.com/docs/transactions.html#note-about-parallelism-in-transactions).

### 3. Daily cleanup cannot drain normal upload and notification bursts

- **What this is:** `vercel.json:3` schedules only `/api/cron/voice-cleanup` once daily. That route calls reservation expiry, storage deletion and email workers with their default **25-record** limits (`lib/storageProtocol.ts:508,638`, `lib/emailOutbox.ts:76`).
- **Problem:** Fifty successful PDF submissions enqueue fifty staging-object deletions, already exceeding one day's default deletion capacity. Fifty abandoned reservations also exceed one expiry pass; queued cancellations retain quota until deletion, and email notifications can wait until the next daily run plus backlog. These source-confirmed limits were not exercised by the short baseline.
- **Fix:** Run the existing workers more frequently where the hosting plan permits, and process repeated bounded batches within an execution-time budget, stopping when empty. Separate notification delivery from long audio cleanup using the existing worker; measure incoming jobs versus drained jobs and retain leases/retries. Match the schedule to actual platform execution limits.
- **If we skip it:** Backlog and stored/reserved bytes grow during busy periods, eventually blocking uploads even when abandoned files should have been removed.

This is a sustained-load capacity problem, not the explanation for every reservation delay in the initial burst. Merely changing 25 to 100 moves the limit; it does not guarantee the backlog drains.

## Should fix

### 4. One-file cleanup fetches unrelated references from five collections

- **What this is:** `lib/storageReferenceSafety.ts:32–72` reads all other nonempty project PDFs, VoiceNote URLs, supervisor audio broadcasts, student audio messages, and Viva snapshot PDF URLs, then normalizes and compares them in JavaScript.
- **Problem:** Resetting/replacing a single PDF repeats these five broad reads inside each mutation's transaction. Team joins, supervisor/academic changes, audio replacements and cron cleanup call the same helper; projecting only the URL still transfers every matching URL. The work grows with total references rather than the candidate files; this is current-source confirmed and a strong mixed-load bottleneck candidate.
- **Fix:** Normalize legacy URL references once through the existing audit/repair workflow, then query the exact candidate keys with `$in`, preserving every exclusion and Viva snapshot check. Add only the supporting exact-key indexes justified by query plans, and avoid refetching the same candidates within one transaction.
- **If we skip it:** Independent small actions repeatedly read unrelated portal data, extend transactions, and occupy the same database pool that dashboards need.

Do not drop shared-reference checks. Until legacy URL forms have been normalized and verified, retain a compatible fallback for those records; equality on the normalized key alone could miss a shared legacy URL.

### 5. PDF reservations and finalizations contend on one quota document

- **What this is:** `lib/storageProtocol.ts:159–223` increments the shared storage ledger for every reservation; lines 398–433 move reserved bytes to used bytes for every finalization. Audio and deletion workers use the same ledger.
- **Problem:** Distinct owners and files still write the same record. Reservations read ledger/reservation state before that write; finalization performs the project commit callback before the ledger update, so a conflict can replay substantial database work. The contention point is confirmed; its exact retry count and share of the 11-second reservation p95 were not recorded.
- **Fix:** Keep quota and reservation state atomic. Remove the separate readiness read on write paths only by folding equivalent numeric/existence/capacity guards into the existing conditional ledger update, backed by the unique `configKey` index and preflight checks; trim the transaction callback and measure retries before/after.
- **If we skip it:** Concurrent uploads keep competing for a global write, and retry overhead grows with the amount of work in each callback.

There is no honest one-line change that eliminates serialization while preserving an exact global quota. Moving the shared update earlier may reduce work replayed before a conflict but can hold the write lock longer; benchmark that ordering separately. Splitting the update outside the transaction needs crash-safe compensation and is more complex than the first fixes. [MongoDB write-conflict behavior](https://www.mongodb.com/docs/manual/core/transactions-production-consideration/#in-progress-transactions-and-write-conflicts).

### 6. Submission also writes a global policy counter

- **What this is:** `app/api/dashboard/student/route.ts:645–654` increments `projectSubmissionsAccepted` on the singleton registration policy for every PDF submission. Registration similarly increments `registrationsAccepted` in `app/api/register/route.ts:156`.
- **Problem:** Submission transactions acquire another shared write after rereading student/project state and checking duplicate titles. Unrelated projects and even registrations can contend on the same policy record; replacing documents for already-submitted projects also increments it. The write is a deliberate closure safeguard, not a harmless statistics update that can simply be removed.
- **Fix:** Replace submission's `findOneAndUpdate` with `updateOne` and check `matchedCount`, keeping the same conditional filter and counter increment: the caller only needs to know whether the gate matched. Put that gate as early as its project-state dependency allows and shorten the work after it; retain the policy returned by registration because registration uses its fine settings.
- **If we skip it:** Submission has two shared transactional write points, making otherwise independent student actions queue or replay.

Replacing this gate with a cached read would change correctness when an admin closes submissions concurrently. Accept that some coordination is necessary; reduce work around it before designing a new gate system.

### 7. Many operations share ten database connections and an unlimited checkout wait

- **What this is:** `lib/mongodb.ts:32` caps each application pool at 10. No `waitQueueTimeoutMS` is configured.
- **Problem:** Hundreds of reads/writes compete for those connections. Earlier diagnostic samples measured mean checkout waiting around 180 ms and a peak interval p95 of 999 ms; those are 6 October measurements, while the same pool limit still exists. Server-selection and socket timeouts do not bound waiting for an available connection.
- **Fix:** Reduce query count, scans and transaction retries first, then compare modest pool sizes on the actual deployment. Configure a measured checkout deadline with a deliberate overload response and bounded client retry; budget connections across processes and database topology.
- **If we skip it:** A query that is quick once sent can still wait behind other work, and a multi-step request can encounter that wait repeatedly.

The earlier pool-10 versus pool-40 comparison worsened dashboard/login p95, so increasing the limit is not an established solution. [MongoDB connection-pool behavior](https://www.mongodb.com/docs/drivers/node/current/connect/connection-options/connection-pools/).

### 8. Concurrent password operations queue for native workers and CPU

- **What this is:** `lib/security/password.ts:9–24` uses asynchronous scrypt with `N=32768`, `r=8`, `p=1`. Login, registration, password completion and student email verification share that work.
- **Problem:** The earlier isolated four-worker experiment took about 152 ms for one check, but roughly 1.97–2.30 seconds to finish fifty checks without HTTP/database work. Native scrypt uses Node's worker pool; the 12 October server's actual worker setting was not saved in its report, so it must not be assumed to be four.
- **Fix:** Keep the password cost and native async implementation. Benchmark startup worker settings such as 4 versus 8 against the actual CPU/memory allocation, then use the smallest setting that improves the complete mixed workload; scale compute only if that workload still exceeds capacity.
- **If we skip it:** Login/reset bursts wait for password workers, and their CPU demand competes with portal work on a shared host.

Eight concurrent derivations need roughly twice the working memory of four; more workers are not free capacity. The worker setting must be applied before Node starts. [Node.js worker-pool documentation](https://nodejs.org/api/cli.html#uv_threadpool_sizesize).

### 9. Upload validation rereads account, project and policy data

- **What this is:** `/api/upload` authenticates User, then rereads it for fine fields at `app/api/upload/route.ts:66`. It reads the project, while `getTeamFineRestriction` rereads that project for members (`lib/teamFineRestriction.ts:62`); outstanding fines cause a second policy read at upload line 108.
- **Problem:** The reservation path can perform 13 logical database operations before URL signing. Submission repeats preflight checks, then correctly rechecks mutable project state in its transaction. Repeated preflight fetches are avoidable; transactional revalidation is not automatically duplication to delete.
- **Fix:** Request fine fields through the existing `requireCurrentUser` extra projection, include members in the initial project projection, and use `getTeamFineRestrictionFromMembers` with one member read. Reuse the policy already loaded within the request; keep authoritative checks needed at commit.
- **If we skip it:** Each concurrent upload adds unnecessary round trips and pool demand before touching the file.

The same principle applies to large hydrated User reads in join/leave/message mutation paths: select the fields actually inspected or changed, while preserving session/version guards. Repeated `connectToDatabase()` calls already return the cached connection; they are not repeated network connections.

### 10. The browser waits for refreshes before confirming saved actions, and PDF retries start over

- **What this is:** `components/student/hooks/useStudentProjectSubmission.ts:155–177` waits for upload, submission, local draft cleanup and a full dashboard refresh before showing success. `uploadStudentPdf` creates a new UUID each call (`components/student/api/studentDashboardApi.ts:151`); reset/team/supervisor and supervisor review hooks also await refreshes before confirmation.
- **Problem:** A saved action still looks unfinished while another dashboard request waits; a failed refresh can be presented as a failed mutation. Retrying a failed submission can reserve and transfer the same PDF again, consume another quota reservation and rate-limit attempt, and leave abandoned staging objects. The HTTP baseline does not include these browser refresh/draft delays.
- **Fix:** Separate mutation success from refresh failure and keep existing data visible during refresh. Retain the reservation idempotency key and uploaded staging key for retries of the same file/attempt, retry finalization after a completed PUT, and reset them on file change/expiry; verify ambiguous completion before resubmitting.
- **If we skip it:** Users wait longer than the API mutation itself and may repeat work that already succeeded, amplifying load.

Reuse the existing hooks and server mutation response. A new client cache framework is unnecessary. Retry the original staging key, not an arbitrary finalized PDF key; finalized-reservation responses must not silently apply a different project-edit request.

### 11. The three-item Viva preview still builds every matching workspace

- **What this is:** `getVivaPanelSessions(actorId, true)` excludes completed sessions but has no limit (`lib/vivaSessionDashboard.ts:974–1042`). The route takes `.slice(0, 3)` only after all workspaces have been assembled (`app/api/dashboard/supervisor/viva/route.ts:36`).
- **Problem:** A supervisor with many scheduled sessions still fetches all session snapshots and live projects/participants to display three titles. Staff messages similarly return every matching current message (`app/api/dashboard/messages/route.ts:213`), and the full Viva workspace mixes active sessions with unbounded completed history. These paths were not capacity-tested by the baseline's one-session-per-panel dashboard fixture.
- **Fix:** Fetch preview candidates in small ordered batches and stop after three valid entries, preserving snapshot/live validation; do not blindly limit to three before validation if invalid entries could hide later valid ones. Page staff messages and completed Viva history with the existing cursor pattern when those lists are large.
- **If we skip it:** Response bytes may be small while database and assembly work still grows with the supervisor's or portal's history.

Supervisor project reads are bounded in normal use by the existing 30–40 project capacity. A new paginated supervisor Home is lower priority than the unbounded Viva/message paths.

### 12. Operational admin Viva reloads still read all historical rounds, panels and schedules

- **What this is:** `lib/vivaRoundAdmin.ts:397–430` loads all rounds, then teams/examiners selected across those rounds; `getVivaPanels()` and `getVivaSchedules()` load all panels/schedules (`lib/vivaPanelAdmin.ts:203`, `lib/vivaScheduling.ts:1604`).
- **Problem:** `catalogue=0` trims the setup catalogue but not the operational history. Canceling/changing one session followed by a UI reload can fetch every round and schedule, including old/cancelled attempts; the API baseline times cancellation itself without this UI reload.
- **Fix:** Add selected-round scope to the existing endpoint and its panel/schedule reads, reuse unchanged catalogue/round data in the mounted workspace, and fetch older history when requested. Keep cross-round overlap queries in schedule validation, where they are needed for correctness.
- **If we skip it:** Admin dashboard refresh cost grows every term even when today's operation affects one session.

### 13. Runtime, seed and deployment index definitions disagree

- **What this is:** `scripts/refactor-indexes.mjs` includes a partial Project `pdfUrl` index not declared in `models/Project.ts`. `scripts/seed-local.mjs:139` calls `syncIndexes()` from model declarations; the deployment catalogue also omits several snapshot/overlap indexes declared on VivaSession.
- **Problem:** Seeding can remove script-only indexes, and preflight can pass without checking every hot-path model index. Exact-key PDF authorization in `lib/security/storage.ts:22` can scan projects for an admin if `pdfUrl` indexing is absent; snapshot reference fields also lack declared exact-key indexes. The divergence is confirmed; actual deployed index absence/plans were not measured in this audit.
- **Fix:** Keep required declarations and the existing index audit/apply list consistent, assert the index set in the disposable fixture, and capture `explain('executionStats')` for hot reads. Add only necessary key/sort indexes, then disable request-process `autoIndex`/`autoCreate` after deployment provisioning is reliable.
- **If we skip it:** Local tests and deployed systems may exercise different plans, and startup can do unnecessary index setup during traffic.

Do not blindly add an index for every filter. The five broad reference reads in finding 4 still return all references even if an index can help their scans; fix their query shape first. Model import/index work was observed in older cold-start driver artifacts, not quantified in the current baseline. [Mongoose index initialization guidance](https://mongoosejs.com/docs/guide.html#autoIndex).

### 14. Search filters and exact totals still require broad database work

- **What this is:** `lib/projectReviewQueue.ts:153–189` runs substring, case-insensitive searches across multiple User fields and returns matching IDs, then searches project text fields. Admin student status pages join/filter all candidates before the facet's page limit (`app/api/admin/students/route.ts:178–223`); every page recomputes an exact filtered total.
- **Problem:** A small returned page does not imply a small query. Broad search terms create large matching-ID lists, while status counting repeats relationship work across pages. The default list already uses cursors; the remaining costs are source-confirmed, but absent from the 12 October workload.
- **Fix:** Preserve search semantics unless a product change is accepted. Use existing exact normalized roll/email fields for identifier searches, retain debounce/cancellation, and measure broad text searches; if name search is a measured bottleneck, add a normalized name field with an indexed prefix query and a verified backfill. Reuse totals for unchanged filters or expose `hasMore` where exact totals are unnecessary.
- **If we skip it:** Searching/page changes can compete with operational requests despite returning only 20–50 rows.

An ordinary case-insensitive name index does not make `/…/i` efficient. [MongoDB regex index guidance](https://www.mongodb.com/docs/manual/reference/operator/query/regex/#index-use).

### 15. Recovery and email-change requests await fresh SMTP connections

- **What this is:** `lib/mailer.ts:24` reuses a transporter but does not enable `pool`. `lib/auth/passwordResetService.ts:109` awaits SMTP and then marks the OTP usable; request handling also deliberately pads fast recovery responses to 750 ms. `lib/auth/studentEmailChange.ts:60,94` awaits verification and change-notice email.
- **Problem:** Reusing a non-pooled transporter does not reuse an SMTP connection for each message; bursts pay connection/protocol overhead. Production SMTP may take much longer than the local capture server, and the post-change notice can delay a change already committed. This is a source-backed production latency candidate, not a measured Brevo delay.
- **Fix:** Enable Nodemailer's existing bounded SMTP pool and explicit connection/greeting/socket deadlines using provider-compatible limits. Keep OTP delivery/activation ordering and the intentional anti-enumeration padding; move the post-change informational notice to the existing durable EmailOutbox, preserving a dedupe key and reliable enqueue.
- **If we skip it:** Recovery/email changes remain tied to provider round trips, and connection bursts add avoidable overhead.

Do not use detached promises for delivery or make an OTP usable before a successful send. [Nodemailer SMTP pooling](https://nodemailer.com/smtp/pooled).

### 16. Many unrelated actions await one recent-activity document

- **What this is:** `lib/portalActivityLog.ts:21` updates the same `_id` for login, password changes and most project/admin actions. The update prepends and trims a maximum of 100 entries (`lib/portalActivityLogPolicy.ts`).
- **Problem:** Successful mutations wait for another shared write after their main commit. The older diagnostic recorded about 40 ms mean command duration for activity updates; that makes this a secondary candidate, not demonstrated responsibility for multi-second baseline delays. The list is bounded, so it is not an unbounded-memory problem.
- **Fix:** Measure activity-write waiting under the new workload first. If it is material, store one activity per document with an `occurredAt, _id` index, query the latest 100, and prune old entries in the existing cron; preserve the current retention and awaited/durable behavior.
- **If we skip it:** Optional recent activity can become a cross-route write bottleneck even when projects and accounts are unrelated.

Simply dropping the await can lose activity on serverless shutdown. Keep the existing implementation if an isolated measurement shows the change would not matter.

## Nice to have

### 17. Some repeated browser reads and local assembly can be removed cheaply

- **What this is:** Student refresh rebuilds/restores the local project draft (`components/student/hooks/useStudentDashboardData.ts:56`, `useStudentProjectDraft.ts:61`) even for unchanged details; student JSON repeats project details under both `student` and `project`. Supervisor Home preview remounts when returning Home; admin summary refetches when its visibility effect runs.
- **Problem:** Draft cleanup/file restoration adds local work to every refresh, and remounts reissue reads that the mounted parent could retain. Most duplicated JSON here is only a few bounded fields, so this is lower priority than database scans and contention; browser duration was not measured.
- **Fix:** Restore local drafts on initial load or a changed project identity, keep API refresh separate from draft restoration, and deduplicate in-flight reads with refs already used elsewhere. Retain Home preview/summary in the parent for the current session with explicit invalidation; remove duplicate payload fields only when updating their existing consumers together.
- **If we skip it:** Actions do extra local/browser work and navigation can refetch information unnecessarily.

### 18. Bulk panel creation issues one save per panel

- **What this is:** `lib/vivaPanelAdmin.ts:381–386` calls `VivaPanel.create(array, { session, ordered: true })`. Older driver artifacts show 75 panel inserts for a 75-panel setup.
- **Problem:** Large panel setup performs many sequential saves inside a transaction. This is an infrequent setup cost and was outside the supplied timed phases; it should not be blamed for routine dashboard delays.
- **Fix:** Use one `insertMany` only after preserving duplicate-examiner and panel-admin validation currently enforced by the schema's validation hook. Validate documents explicitly if necessary, and compare a 100/150-panel save before choosing this change.
- **If we skip it:** Larger setup saves keep more database round trips than necessary.

## PDF upload: what to improve first

The existing upload architecture is appropriate: the browser sends bytes directly to storage using a signed PUT. A signed URL is generated locally with the configured credentials; reservation latency is not the time to ask R2 to upload or sign a document. [Cloudflare R2 presigned URL documentation](https://developers.cloudflare.com/r2/api/s3/presigned-urls/).

The successful path is:

```mermaid
sequenceDiagram
    participant B as Browser
    participant A as Portal API
    participant D as MongoDB
    participant S as Object storage
    B->>A: POST /api/upload (metadata)
    A->>D: Account/access, policy, fines, rate limits
    A->>D: Transaction: reserve quota + upload record
    A-->>B: Signed PUT URL + staging key
    B->>S: PUT PDF directly
    B->>A: POST submitProject (staging key + details)
    A->>D: Account/access and submission checks
    A->>S: HEAD staging object
    A->>S: GET first eight bytes
    A->>S: Conditional COPY to immutable final key
    A->>D: Transaction: project, policy gate, quota, reservation, email/deletion outboxes
    A->>D: Recent activity update
    A-->>B: Submission saved
    B->>A: GET full student dashboard
    Note over B: Draft cleanup/restoration and refresh precede current success message
```

`lib/storageProtocol.ts:316–439` confirms three storage round trips during submission: HEAD, an eight-byte range GET, and COPY. The COPY uses the verified object's ETag to protect against overwriting the staging object while its PUT URL is still usable. The database transaction begins after those storage calls, which is already a good boundary.

| Order | Small change | Why this is the appropriate first step |
| --- | --- | --- |
| 1 | Fix candidate-key reference queries and duplicated preflight reads (4, 9) | Removes unrelated database work with the existing helper and models |
| 2 | Shorten ledger/policy transactions and count retries (5, 6) | Addresses coordination around the baseline's slowest upload API steps |
| 3 | Keep the same upload attempt across retries; confirm save before refresh (10) | Prevents repeated reservation/transfer and avoids waiting for refresh to acknowledge a save |
| 4 | Drain abandoned reservations and staging deletions reliably (3) | Prevents quota leakage/backlog after bursts |
| 5 | Measure realistic object transfer and each storage round trip | Identifies whether production network/storage latency needs a separate change |

Do not proxy PDFs through Next.js, introduce multipart upload for a 4 MiB limit, compress PDFs on the API, or remove storage validation/conditional promotion. Those changes either add work/complexity or weaken current guarantees. The current server only reads eight signature bytes; it does not parse or recompress the PDF using pdf-lib during upload.

If storage verification is later measured as material, HEAD and signature GET could potentially be replaced with one bounded range GET that validates the total size from `Content-Range`, content type and ETag. Test R2 and MinIO behavior, malformed/missing headers, and object changes first; never treat the eight-byte response length as the whole file size. This is a secondary optimization, not the demonstrated root of the local reservation delay.

## Changes that are already present and should not be proposed again

- Existing policy reads no longer upsert the registration document merely to view a dashboard.
- The student dashboard passes one executed project promise into the scheduling fallback and returns early when no sessions exist.
- Admin Home defers student/supervisor management lists; later student pages can omit global metadata.
- Reports load the selected report and retain successful selections in a native Map; superseded requests use AbortController.
- The student supervisor catalogue is loaded when needed, and template bundles are cached in the process and browser.
- Viva panel reads batch related people/projects and use snapshots for running/completed sessions; there is no per-session User query loop in that dashboard path.
- Project notifications and file deletion are durable outbox work, rather than synchronous SMTP/deletion in the submission transaction.
- S3 and MongoDB clients are reused. Public headline/policy/supervisor content has a 60-second cache, and maintenance reads have a five-second coalesced cache.

These checks prevent repeating stale conclusions from the older reports. Broad caching of private dashboards, fine checks, revocation or transaction gates is not the first fix: it would introduce invalidation and freshness problems without removing the root query work.

## Verification needed to rank the remaining causes precisely

Use the existing disposable build/database/bucket workflow; record the code revision, actual index list, pool/worker settings and sanitized environment metadata. The current endpoint timings are insufficient for a percentage breakdown of root causes.

1. Add sampled, identifier-free phase durations to the existing diagnostic approach: auth, pool checkout, command time, retry count, mapping/serialization, reservation, HEAD/range GET/COPY, commit and activity write. Reuse `LOGIN_PHASE_TIMINGS` and the review route's `Server-Timing`; avoid logging credentials, tokens, object URLs or PDF content.
2. Capture execution plans for candidate-key checks, PDF authorization, student schedule queries, preview/history and broad admin searches. Record keys/documents examined, returned rows, sort work and elapsed time; compare the same fixture before/after.
3. Repeat the exact 12 October burst for comparability, then a corrected workload where logins actually overlap mixed writes. Include 1 MiB and near-4 MiB PDFs; report reservation, transfer, verification/promotion, commit and browser-refresh durations separately. Do not sum separate p95 values to invent an end-to-end p95.
4. Change one mechanism at a time and repeat measurements: narrowed references, preflight reuse, transaction shortening, then pool/worker settings. Measure CPU, event-loop lag, memory, MongoDB waiting/conflicts and completed throughput alongside latency.
5. Test collisions deliberately: several students selecting one supervisor, simultaneous team joins, gate closure during submissions, one reservation finalized twice, timeout after a successful commit, shared PDFs retained by Viva snapshots, and retries after an expired upload URL.
6. Test 75-way bursts and 150 distinct sessions, then sustained traffic long enough to observe worker backlog. Verify quota/capacity, unique membership, audit counts, delivered emails and cleanup; zero HTTP errors alone is insufficient.

The smallest first implementation is finding 4 plus finding 9, alongside the transaction reliability corrections. They reuse existing helpers and remove work before any tuning. Findings 5–8 require measured comparisons because coordination and finite hardware capacity cannot be removed by adding more concurrency.

**Verdict:** The portal's main load risk is repeated database work competing for limited capacity, with global storage/policy writes amplifying transaction costs. Reduce candidate scans and redundant reads first; repair transaction handling and worker draining, then tune from measurements.

**Lean:** All proposed first fixes use the existing stack; **0 new dependencies** are needed. Several duplicate reads and control-flow branches can be deleted, but an exact line-saving estimate would be invented before implementation. Keep fixes in the current auth, storage, dashboard, worker and index files; no new service layer or queue framework is justified by this evidence.

**Not checked:** No fresh production or local HTTP load run, live database explain/index inspection, browser performance trace, deployed R2/Brevo timing, platform cold-start profiling, or full-day worker test was performed in this audit. No application/database service was started or connected to during this audit. Current routes, models, shared services, browser hooks, seed/index/worker code and relevant tests were inspected; historical diagnostic/report artifacts were checked as supporting evidence. Not every presentation-only component or unit test was read line by line. No claim of exhaustive production root-cause attribution or measured improvement is made.

Suggested report commit message: `docs: audit portal load bottlenecks and PDF upload flow`
</file>

