<file path="docs/portal-performance-diagnosis-2026-10-06.md">
# Portal performance diagnosis — 6 October 2026

This report records the pre-change diagnosis. Approved changes and their measured results are documented in [the implementation and retest report](portal-performance-improvement-2026-10-06.md).

The portal survived the local workload, but response times were slow. The main problem is the amount of database and password-checking work competing for limited application capacity. Application fixes have not been implemented.

## Evidence and proposed fixes

| Priority | Finding | Evidence | Smallest useful fix |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| 1 | Viewing a student dashboard writes registration settings unnecessarily. | `lib/features/student/dashboard.ts:33` calls `getOrCreateRegistrationPolicy`; `lib/registrationPolicy.ts:11` always runs an upsert. Schema timestamps turn this into an update even when the policy exists. Diagnostic snapshots recorded 450 policy find-and-modify commands. | Use the existing read helper; create defaults only when the policy is missing. Preserve policy changes and closure checks. |
| 2 | Student dashboards repeat database work. | The dashboard loads a project, while `getConfirmedVivaSchedulesForStudent` separately loads it again when no schedule snapshot exists. That fallback also loads rounds, sessions and panels, including panels when there are no matching sessions. | Reuse the project within the request and stop unnecessary fallback queries early. Preserve schedules for older records without snapshots. |
| 3 | Database operations queue for ten available connections. | `lib/mongodb.ts:32` limits the pool to ten. During the instrumented baseline, connection checkout averaged approximately 180 ms; the highest ten-second interval p95 was 999 ms. Most individual command averages were about 26–42 ms. | Reduce operations first, then benchmark modest pool sizes on the intended hosting environment. Consider bounded waiting with deliberate overload handling; a timeout alone does not improve capacity. |
| 4 | Admin viva reads load the entire project catalogue. | `lib/vivaRoundAdmin.ts:397` loads all populated projects and their students, all rounds, panels and schedules. The fixture contained 2,500 projects and about 4,900 project members, although the round had 75 sessions. | Separate current-round data from selection catalogues; search/page the catalogue and load it when needed. |
| 5 | Concurrent logins queue behind expensive password checks. | The application uses asynchronous scrypt. An isolated single check took about 152 ms, but a wave of 50 took 1.97–2.30 seconds with four workers, before any database or HTTP work. Mixed diagnostic snapshots observed up to 36 outstanding checks. | Keep password strength. Benchmark worker counts against CPU and memory limits after reducing request work; scale application capacity if necessary. |

The application event loop was busy approximately 87% of the sampled baseline window, sometimes effectively 100%. This confirms application pressure, but does not identify one specific CPU function. No stack-level CPU profile was collected, so serialization, instrumentation or any particular library should not be declared the dominant CPU cause.

## A larger connection pool was not a successful fix

Two instrumented 90-second local runs used the same workload configuration and temporary monitoring. Only the application pool setting changed, from 10 to 40; no repository configuration was changed.

| Mixed-traffic p95 | Pool 10 | Pool 40 |
| --- | ---: | ---: |
| Student dashboard | 7.77 s | 9.01 s |
| Login, CSRF plus callback | 7.48 s | 9.34 s |
| Supervisor viva dashboard | 2.95 s | 5.19 s |

Both runs passed correctness checks. Some team operations improved with the larger pool, but important dashboard and login tail latencies worsened. These are single sequential comparisons, not repeated randomized trials; they establish that blindly increasing the pool is not a demonstrated solution. The workload waits for responses before repeating, so completed operation counts differ between runs.

An isolated four-versus-eight-worker scrypt comparison showed a modest, variable improvement, not proof that eight workers would speed up the complete portal. More workers also consume additional memory and CPU.

## Secondary suspects and limits

- Every login and several student mutations await an update to one shared recent-activity document (`lib/portalActivityLog.ts`). This concentrates writes. Its measured command average was about 40 ms, so it is a secondary candidate, not the demonstrated main bottleneck. If further isolation proves contention, use individual activity records while preserving durable logging.
- Five checked query plans used indexes efficiently. This does not prove every query is indexed, but there is no basis to blame missing indexes generally.
- The load test deliberately repeated dashboards during active sessions more frequently than the current UI normally does. The UI does not contain the continuous five-second dashboard polling simulated by the test.
- The local test did not measure browser rendering, deployed network latency or actual hosting limits. Local results cannot certify production capacity.
- Atlas Free limits are a separate deployment constraint, not the cause of local queueing. Validate the intended database tier before making a production capacity claim.
- The previously discovered PDF closure bypass after a project reset is a separate correctness issue; it does not explain the measured slowness.

## Recommended approval scope

First implement read-only policy retrieval and remove duplicate/empty dashboard queries. Then reduce admin payloads. Retest the same mixed workload and test 75 simultaneously active sessions with roughly 75 concurrent logins to check the requested 50% headroom. Only then choose connection-pool, worker-count or hosting changes from measurements. These first fixes reuse existing code and need no new dependencies.

## Sources and artifacts

- [MongoDB connection-pool documentation](https://www.mongodb.com/docs/drivers/node/current/connect/connection-options/connection-pools/) explains connection waiting and the default unlimited wait-queue timeout.
- [Node.js CLI documentation source](https://github.com/nodejs/node/blob/main/doc/api/cli.md) documents scrypt's shared worker pool and its default size of four.
- Previous full-run report: `docs/viva-mixed-stress-report-2026-10-06.md`.
- Raw diagnostic artifacts, excluded from Git: `.local-bench/root-causes-2026-10-06/`, including baseline/pool-40 HTTP results, driver timing snapshots and isolated scrypt results.
- Monitoring adds overhead; the earlier uninstrumented ten-minute run remains the primary traffic benchmark. Ten-second diagnostic boundaries can include adjacent phases, so aggregated driver timings are approximate.

The temporary Next servers were stopped and the dedicated synthetic stress database was removed after testing. No application behavior or production data was changed by this diagnosis.
</file>

<file path="docs/portal-performance-improvement-2026-10-06.md">
# Portal performance changes and local retest — 6 October 2026

The approved fixes are implemented without new dependencies. The tables below show the observed changes on this local machine, including regressions. These results do not establish production capacity.

## What changed

- `getOrCreateRegistrationPolicy` first uses the existing read helper. Only a missing policy triggers initialization. Existing policy reads no longer write `updatedAt`; changed restrictions remain immediately visible.
- Student dashboard requests reuse one executed project query. The scheduling fallback now checks that project's sessions through a new index before reading rounds or panels. Snapshot-based and legacy schedules remain supported, and unconfirmed schedules remain hidden.
- The admin operational endpoint returns teams and examiners already selected for rounds. The existing setup UI explicitly requests the full catalogue, preserving program quotas and bulk selection. Switching sections cancels stale effect requests.
- The new `projectId, scheduledAt, _id` session index is declared in the model and the existing index audit/apply catalogue. No production index or data was changed.
- The existing stress runner now supports 75 active sessions, student writers and login bursts for the headroom test. Generated benchmark artifacts are excluded from lint.

Password cost, four password workers, the ten-connection application pool, authentication, rate limits, transactions and audit logging remained enabled and unchanged in the measured runs.

## Matched ten-minute local comparison

Both runs used production-mode Next.js, the same loopback MongoDB replica set and MinIO settings, 5,501 initial users, 2,500 initial projects, 75 scheduled sessions, 50 simultaneous start requests, 50 student writers and alternating 40/50-user login bursts. Names and batches changed once; joining, resetting and leaving repeated. The submissions switch stayed closed and no PDF files were transferred.

This is one before/final-after pair on a shared i5-8250U machine with 7.61 GiB RAM. Builds, unit tests and browser checks were kept outside measured load windows. The new index was initialized before load. The earlier ten-minute report is a different run; use this fresh baseline for the comparison.

p95 means 95% of measurements completed within that time. Login timing combines CSRF and credential callback, excluding the separately measured session-identity request and browser rendering.

| Operation | Before p95 | Final after p95 | Observed change |
| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| Concurrent viva start | 1.50 s | 1.29 s | 13.9% lower |
| Login (CSRF + callback) | 10.81 s | 9.26 s | 14.4% lower |
| Student dashboard | 11.43 s | 7.38 s | 35.4% lower |
| Supervisor viva dashboard | 9.19 s | 4.45 s | 51.6% lower |
| Admin operational viva dashboard | 12.83 s | 13.54 s | 5.5% higher |
| Grade save | 12.50 s | 11.33 s | 9.3% lower |
| Name change | 9.24 s | 7.57 s | 18.0% lower |
| Batch change | 9.01 s | 4.37 s | 51.6% lower |
| Join team | 9.66 s | 7.01 s | 27.4% lower |
| Reset project | 4.03 s | 3.16 s | 21.4% lower |
| Leave team | 3.97 s | 3.34 s | 15.8% lower |

| Operation | Before median | Final after median |
| --- | ---: | ---: |
| Login (CSRF + callback) | 3.34 s | 3.22 s |
| Student dashboard | 4.19 s | 2.61 s |
| Grade save | 2.88 s | 2.42 s |
| Join team | 2.31 s | 1.78 s |
| Leave team | 1.07 s | 0.97 s |

The baseline served **22,774 actual mixed-phase HTTP requests** over 616.36 seconds, approximately **36.95/s**. The final run served **21,215** over 628.93 seconds, approximately **33.73/s**: **8.7% lower throughput**. The combined CSRF/callback metric is excluded from request totals because it does not issue additional requests.

The workload waits for responses before repeating, so completed request counts change as response times change. Elapsed times include final loop waits and in-flight work after the planned 600-second window. These are observed averages, not a fixed-arrival-rate capacity ceiling.

Both runs reported 0 and 0 unexpected HTTP responses respectively, and passed their data checks. The final run recorded 1,800 accepted grade saves with exactly matching audit events. Stale grades were rejected, competing joins had one winner, all 50 vivas completed, participant locks cleared, memberships remained unique, and supervisor capacity counters matched projects.

An intermediate ten-minute run reduced p95 for several operations but lowered aggregate throughput by 9.1%. It is retained as `after-first-pass`; it prompted the additional indexed scheduling shortcut. Variation between these runs reinforces that latency percentages are observations, not guaranteed gains.

## Fifty-percent headroom check

The final build ran **75 simultaneous viva starts**, **75 student writers** and **60/75-user login bursts**, with 5,551 initial users and 2,550 initial projects. The planned load window was 180 seconds and drained in 188.33 seconds. It served 9,452 actual mixed-phase HTTP requests, approximately 50.19/s.

Status: **passed**; unexpected HTTP responses: **0**. Accepted grade saves: 900. This short headroom check establishes only the observed local result, not ten-minute or full-day reliability at 75 sessions.

Its throughput should not be compared directly with the ten-minute runs: duration, login counts and workload mix differ.

| Operation | Headroom p95 | Headroom maximum |
| --- | ---: | ---: |
| Concurrent viva start | 1.63 s | 1.64 s |
| Login (CSRF + callback) | 5.76 s | 6.25 s |
| Student dashboard | 7.54 s | 8.88 s |
| Supervisor viva dashboard | 4.16 s | 6.27 s |
| Grade save | 7.86 s | 8.31 s |
| Join team | 8.43 s | 11.43 s |
| Reset project | 6.93 s | 8.09 s |
| Leave team | 5.21 s | 9.01 s |

## Verification and remaining limits

- Production webpack build and TypeScript checks passed; lint passed; all 74 unit-test files passed.
- Local integration checks passed: policy initialization, zero writes during concurrent reads of an existing policy, immediate restriction changes, full setup catalogue, reduced operational catalogue, legacy schedules, unconfirmed schedules, project reuse and empty-result short circuits.
- The new empty scheduling query used an index and examined zero unrelated documents in the regression fixture.
- Chromium verified setup → panels → schedule → setup with valid responses and no uncaught page errors. The retained fixture's full catalogue response was about 864 KB versus 82 KB operationally, approximately 90.5% fewer JSON bytes. This is uncompressed JSON size, not a browser-rendering benchmark.
- The previously reported PDF-closure bypass after a project reset remains a separate correctness issue. Closing the switch and sending no PDFs does not prove every upload path is blocked.
- Database tier limits, deployment network latency, cloud cold starts, PDF object deletion costs and full-day behavior remain outside this test. Settings should be tuned against deployment-matched staging measurements.

The source changes reduce unnecessary work, but response times in these tables remain the actual basis for judging responsiveness. Passing correctness checks alone does not mean the portal is comfortably fast under peak traffic.

The new session index is included in the existing `indexes:refactor:audit` and `indexes:refactor:apply` workflow. Review and build it during the normal deployment process before relying on the optimized lookup. No production deployment was performed.

## Artifacts and replay

Raw reports, comparison JSON, browser results/screenshots and source patch are in the gitignored `.local-bench/improvement-2026-10-06/` directory. The test server and dedicated synthetic database were cleaned up after verification.

With a production server configured for the same isolated local database and loopback authentication URL:

```bash
VIVA_STRESS_BASE_URL=http://localhost:3102 \
VIVA_STRESS_MONGODB_URI='mongodb://127.0.0.1:27017/fyp_viva_http_stress_test?replicaSet=rs0' \
VIVA_MIXED_SECONDS=600 \
VIVA_STRESS_ARTIFACT_DIR=.local-bench/replay-50 \
npm run test:viva:stress:mixed
```

For headroom, set `VIVA_MIXED_ACTIVE_SESSIONS=75` and `VIVA_MIXED_SECONDS=180`. The runner validates loopback and the dedicated database name, and drops only that synthetic database. Keep external telemetry and email blocked/disabled as in these runs.

Suggested commit message: `perf: reduce dashboard queries and viva admin payloads`
</file>

<file path="docs/viva-mixed-stress-report-2026-10-06.md">
# Local viva mixed-traffic test — October 6, 2026

The requested workload completed without unexpected HTTP responses, timeouts, or detected data inconsistencies. Responsiveness degraded substantially: student dashboard p95 was 7.02 seconds, login p95 was 6.79 seconds, and grade-save p95 was 5.73 seconds. This demonstrates local correctness under this workload, not comfortable response times or production capacity.

The separate PDF restriction probe found a functional gap: closing submissions does not disable every upload. Resetting a never-submitted project increases its version to 1, after which the upload API issues a signed URL despite submissions remaining closed. No PDF was transferred in any test.

## Environment and method

- Current application source: commit `bf5bb66`; only test tooling, the package test command, and this report were changed. A fresh `next build --webpack` succeeded. Next.js ran in production mode on loopback port 3102.
- Node 24.18.0, Next.js 16.3.5, Mongoose 9.10.2, NextAuth 4.24.15; local MongoDB 7.0.41, single-node replica set `rs0`.
- Intel Core i5-8250U, 8 logical CPUs, 7.61 GiB RAM. The app, database, test client, and resource sampler shared this machine.
- Synthetic fixture: 5,501 users and 2,500 projects initially. Exactly 4,900 students had project memberships; the two login-only fixture accounts did not have projects.
- The admin API created a round, saved 75 two-examiner panels, scheduled 75 distinct teams, and confirmed the round. The admin submissions switch was closed before starting the vivas.
- Fifty start requests were sent concurrently; all succeeded. Fifty sessions stayed active while the remaining 25 stayed scheduled.
- Fifty unrelated students each changed their name and batch once, respecting the one-change-per-day policies, then repeatedly joined a paired student's team, reset that project, and left it.
- Independent loops issued 40/50-user successful login bursts, 50 grade saves, and 50 student plus 50 viva dashboard reads. The admin viva dashboard was also read. Login waves waited 15 seconds after completing; writer cycles waited 2 seconds, dashboard cycles 5 seconds, and grade cycles 10 seconds.
- The load window was 600 seconds; in-flight operations and loop waits drained in 606.17 seconds. The full run, including setup and final checks, lasted from 19:20:47 to 19:31:20 PKT.
- Requests had a 30-second timeout. Authentication, origin checks, access restrictions, password hashing, transaction handling, and the application's pool size of 10 remained enabled. Sentry tracing was set to zero, outbound sockets were restricted to loopback, and real email credentials were disabled.

## Results

The mixed phase served **26,450 real HTTP requests**, approximately **43.6 requests/second** across the drained window. The synthetic CSRF-plus-callback timing row is excluded from that count because it measures two existing requests rather than issuing another request.

| Operation | Successful requests | Median | p95 | Maximum |
| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| Concurrent viva starts | 50 | 1.17 s | 1.27 s | 1.27 s |
| Login, CSRF + callback | 1,350 | 3.10 s | 6.79 s | 9.00 s |
| Student dashboard | 3,050 | 4.11 s | 7.02 s | 8.90 s |
| Viva dashboard | 3,050 | 1.48 s | 4.17 s | 5.53 s |
| Admin viva dashboard | 61 | 4.54 s | 6.91 s | 7.79 s |
| Grade save | 2,250 | 2.26 s | 5.73 s | 7.30 s |
| Name change | 50 | 8.24 s | 9.10 s | 9.13 s |
| Batch change | 50 | 1.44 s | 3.18 s | 3.24 s |
| Join team | 4,628 | 1.67 s | 5.48 s | 10.09 s |
| Reset project | 4,628 | 0.75 s | 2.57 s | 9.31 s |
| Leave team | 4,628 | 0.96 s | 3.20 s | 9.99 s |
| Complete viva after load | 50 | 0.41 s | 0.68 s | 0.69 s |

p95 means 95% of the measured operations completed within that time. There were **30 login waves and 1,350/1,350 successful logins** during active vivas. A separate pre-viva 50-login wave had p95 3.89 seconds; this single-run comparison is context, not a controlled optimization experiment. Name and batch statistics describe one initial synchronized burst each, not continuous repeated changes.

Correctness checks passed: all 50 vivas completed; 25 remained unstarted; all participant locks were released; accepted grade saves had exactly 2,250 matching audit events; stale grades could not overwrite valid grades; all 4,900 project-member students retained exactly one team; teams were nonempty and within capacity; supervisor occupied-slot counters matched actual projects; student names and batches matched their accepted updates; and storage used/reserved bytes and upload reservation counts remained zero throughout the main test.

Ten students competed for one vacant team slot after the load phase: exactly one joined and nine received the expected 409 response. The name cooldown rejected repeat changes with 429, and a student with an active viva was correctly denied both a detail change and a fresh login. Expected rejections were counted separately from failures.

## Resource evidence and interpretation

Sixty approximately ten-second samples within the mixed phase recorded Next server RSS of **596–845 MiB**, MongoDB resident memory of **221–282 MiB**, and Next server CPU of **94–197%** (100% represents one logical CPU). MongoDB had 42–44 open connections, zero rejected connections, and zero sampled database lock queue. These snapshots do not measure the application's connection-pool wait queue and cannot exclude brief queues between samples.

Server-wide MongoDB insert/query/update/delete counter deltas averaged **272 operations/second**, ranging from 196 to 327 over sample intervals. Aggregation and other commands are excluded, so this is an approximate CRUD workload indicator rather than an exact Atlas billing/quota measure. The server-wide write-conflict counter increased by 672; no resulting API failures or final inconsistencies were detected. The fixture initialization and database cleanup were excluded from these resource ranges.

Five read-only query plans for login lookup, student project lookup, participant restrictions, examiner panels, and snapshot schedules used indexes and examined zero or one documents. Those five paths do not cover every query. Internal timing across the full run's 1,502 accepted setup/baseline/load logins recorded mean password verification of 718 ms out of 1,777 ms total authorization time. Password work and queued database round trips are plausible contributors; this test did not isolate a single root cause. The shared activity-feed document is another contention candidate, not a proven cause.

Local MongoDB has no Atlas Free throughput cap. Atlas Free currently permits 100 read/write operations per second and throttles excess traffic. The observed local workload is therefore a reason to expect substantially worse response times on that tier, not evidence that the same load will pass there. [MongoDB Atlas Free limits](https://www.mongodb.com/docs/atlas/reference/free-shared-limitations/)

## PDF restriction finding

In a separate isolated fixture, the existing admin API closed submissions. A never-submitted project's upload request returned 403 with `PROJECT_SUBMISSIONS_CLOSED`. Calling the real `resetProject` endpoint then raised its version to 1. The identical upload request returned 200 with a signed URL pointing to local MinIO and reserved 100 bytes, even though submissions were still closed. No PUT or PDF transfer occurred; the separate fixture was dropped afterward.

`hasPreviousProjectSubmission()` treats any positive project version as a previous submission, while `resetProject` also increments that version. The existing switch cannot enforce a complete PDF freeze for this scenario. The main capacity test transferred no PDFs and had no upload reservations, but it must not be described as proof that every upload is blocked.

## Scope, validation, and replay

This was one local ten-minute closed-loop API run: each actor waits for its previous response, so arrival rate falls as responses slow. It does not establish a fixed-RPS breaking point, a full-day soak result, browser rendering performance, remote network latency, cloud cold-start behavior, actual PDF deletion costs, email-change delivery capacity, or 75 simultaneously active vivas. Projects contained no PDF objects. No application capacity changes were applied to improve the result.

The short mixed preflight passed after correcting an initial localhost/origin mismatch in the harness setup. The original stress runner's preflight also passed after the helper changes. Fresh production build, focused ESLint, JavaScript syntax checks, diff whitespace checks, and rejection of non-loopback/wrong-database targets passed. The dedicated `fyp_viva_http_stress_test` database was dropped after each run, and the test server and resource sampler were stopped.

The new runner reuses the existing HTTP client, password fixtures, model loader, metrics, and environment guards. It adds no dependencies. With a production server configured for the dedicated local database, replay it with:

```bash
VIVA_STRESS_BASE_URL=http://localhost:3102 \
VIVA_STRESS_MONGODB_URI='mongodb://127.0.0.1:27017/fyp_viva_http_stress_test?replicaSet=rs0' \
VIVA_STRESS_ARTIFACT_DIR=.local-bench/mixed-replay \
npm run test:viva:stress:mixed
```

`VIVA_MIXED_SECONDS` defaults to 600 and accepts 60–1800. `VIVA_MIXED_LARGE_FIXTURE=0` selects the smaller preflight fixture. The server must use the same local database and a matching local authentication URL/secret; the runner only drops the explicitly named test database. External telemetry and email must be disabled or blocked when replaying locally.

Raw evidence is retained in `.local-bench/mixed-2026-10-06/`: the full JSON report, short preflight reports, legacy preflight report, resource samples/summary, five query plans, internal login-phase summary, PDF restriction probe, and server/build/test logs. These generated artifacts are intentionally gitignored.

Recommended next work is a complete PDF-freeze fix and measured tuning of the existing login/database paths, followed by the same workload on deployment-matched staging. A rewrite or new dependency is not justified by this evidence.

Suggested commit message: `test: add strict mixed-traffic viva stress scenario`
</file>

